WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.810 --> 00:00:03.170
My name is Hans Moritz Guenther.

00:00:03.170 --> 00:00:05.870
I'm going to tell you
about how stars are born

00:00:05.870 --> 00:00:08.000
and how we know that.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:10.160
I'm an astronomer.

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:12.630
I don't know who of you is.

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:15.320
And so I want to not just say--

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:17.780
I don't want to just say
what the result is, I want

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:19.970
to show you how we get there.

00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:22.030
And that means that
some of the things I

00:00:22.030 --> 00:00:24.070
show you are not
going to look nice.

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:27.610
And that's because, in practice,
research data doesn't usually

00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:28.660
look like this.

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:31.300
Sometimes it does,
and that's fantastic.

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:34.220
But often, it doesn't.

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:36.428
So I'm going to tell you,
as I said, how stars form.

00:00:36.428 --> 00:00:37.720
I'll tell you how we know that.

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:40.210
And I'll tell you a little
bit about how we actually

00:00:40.210 --> 00:00:42.850
work, like the social part
of being an astronomer.

00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:45.010
How does science come together?

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:46.240
How do we get that data?

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:47.560
Those types of things.

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:50.722
Because I find that that's
often what people like to know

00:00:50.722 --> 00:00:52.180
and I think it's
really interesting

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:53.972
and it's important of
how it goes together.

00:00:53.972 --> 00:00:55.810
But when you read
the New York Times

00:00:55.810 --> 00:00:58.393
about some great new discovery,
it's not going to be in there.

00:00:58.393 --> 00:01:00.880
The pretty picture is
going to be in the--

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:02.530
discovery will be
described there.

00:01:02.530 --> 00:01:05.660
But how people actually got
there is usually not in there.

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:07.040
So I want to tell you that.

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:08.560
So how do stars form?

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:12.730
This is the Orion Nebula
cloud, an optical picture

00:01:12.730 --> 00:01:15.670
of the Orion Nebula cloud
zoomed in a lot, obviously.

00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:17.350
And then, for some
of these pieces,

00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:22.510
like this tiny dot
here and this--

00:01:22.510 --> 00:01:26.173
find my mouse-- this tiny dot
over there zoomed in even more.

00:01:26.173 --> 00:01:28.090
And I'm going to talk
about all these elements

00:01:28.090 --> 00:01:28.882
in a little pieces.

00:01:28.882 --> 00:01:30.970
I just want to tell you
that star forming regions

00:01:30.970 --> 00:01:35.530
are one of the things
that look fantastic.

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:39.220
Now, to orient you a
little bit about what

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:41.020
I'm going to talk
about, here's a cartoon

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:43.300
of the phases of star formation.

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:45.850
Stars form-- I showed you the
picture of the Orion Nebular

00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:49.090
cloud-- in these huge
clouds of gas and dust.

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:50.980
And these huge clouds
of gas and dust

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:52.630
exist in our Milky Way today.

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:54.995
Specifically, if you travel
to the Southern hemisphere

00:01:54.995 --> 00:01:56.620
and you look at the
Milky Way at night,

00:01:56.620 --> 00:01:59.320
there's these Black
patches in the sky.

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:01.630
And that's not because
there's no stars.

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:04.150
Actually, you see on that image
on the wall over there too.

00:02:04.150 --> 00:02:06.250
That's not because
there's no stars.

00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:10.030
It's because there's
clouds of cold gas and dust

00:02:10.030 --> 00:02:12.850
in front of us that cover
up some of those stars.

00:02:12.850 --> 00:02:14.770
And when I say
cold gas and dust,

00:02:14.770 --> 00:02:18.430
I mean, about 20 to 100 Kelvin.

00:02:18.430 --> 00:02:20.810
So that'll be like
minus 200 Fahrenheit.

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:23.980
And when I say dust, it's
like, if you lick your finger

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:25.780
and go like this over
the furniture that

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:28.240
hasn't been cleared, that's
actually pretty similar.

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:30.340
I'll do it outside,
often on this dust,

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:31.910
there's water ice on it.

00:02:31.910 --> 00:02:35.120
So if you go outside right now
and you just grab a snowball

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:37.460
and fizzle it up,
that's actually

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:39.770
pretty similar to what
these clouds of gas

00:02:39.770 --> 00:02:42.230
and dust in the universe are.

00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:44.360
And then, because
these clouds all

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:48.170
depend on little pieces, gas,
particles, and dust clumps

00:02:48.170 --> 00:02:50.000
and gravity contracts.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:52.770
Gravity, all these little
pieces pull on each other

00:02:52.770 --> 00:02:54.800
so they contract
towards the middle.

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:56.900
Now, they don't always
go to the same clump,

00:02:56.900 --> 00:02:59.660
but they could go
to multiple clumps.

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:02.030
And so you form
this inner cloud.

00:03:02.030 --> 00:03:06.230
You form these smaller clump
pieces and these clump pieces

00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:07.670
start contracting.

00:03:07.670 --> 00:03:09.380
And as they start
contracting, they

00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:11.360
get what we call an inner core.

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:14.300
And then, what happens is
when you compress something,

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:15.590
it gets hot.

00:03:15.590 --> 00:03:20.590
If you go and fill up your
bicycle with a hand pump

00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:23.275
and after your tire
is full, you touch

00:03:23.275 --> 00:03:25.150
the valve or you touch
the front of the pump,

00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:26.430
it's going to be hot.

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:30.862
That's because every time you
compress the air, it heats up.

00:03:30.862 --> 00:03:32.320
And so the same
thing happens here.

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:34.608
And eventually, it gets
denser and denser and denser

00:03:34.608 --> 00:03:36.400
and eventually it's
going to get hot enough

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:39.830
that it starts shining
and we can see it.

00:03:39.830 --> 00:03:44.280
So that'll be like this inner
bit here, this inner star here.

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:46.980
Now, these gas clouds, these
clouds of gas and dust,

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:49.110
they're never stationary.

00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:50.250
There's always flows.

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:52.200
There's ebbs and-- not tides.

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:55.210
But there's flow over
here and flow over there

00:03:55.210 --> 00:03:57.240
and there's a big
massive heavy star there

00:03:57.240 --> 00:03:59.020
that pulls material up.

00:03:59.020 --> 00:04:03.150
And so when you compress it
from 200,000 astronomical units

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:05.610
to just one astronomical
unit, the same thing

00:04:05.610 --> 00:04:09.270
happens as if somebody on
the ice pulls their arms in,

00:04:09.270 --> 00:04:13.120
or if you want to speak physics,
angular momentum is conserved.

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:16.110
And so a tiny motion over
here gets a really fast

00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:17.560
spinning motion in the middle.

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:19.980
And because we have this
really fast spinning motion,

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:25.230
around these central
objects, you form a disk.

00:04:25.230 --> 00:04:27.240
And these disks
are the reason why

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:29.970
the planets in our
solar system all

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:32.610
orbit around the sun in the
same way, in the same plane,

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:34.380
because they're all
formed in this disk.

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:36.240
It was random, you
could have one planet

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:38.580
go this way and one planet
go this way and one planet

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:39.370
go that way.

00:04:39.370 --> 00:04:41.790
But that's not what we see
because they're all formed

00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:43.080
in this disk that rotate--

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:45.280
all rotate pretty
flat around the sun.

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:48.730
There's a little bit up and down
because it's not exactly flat.

00:04:48.730 --> 00:04:51.590
But essentially, all
orbit in the same plane.

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:53.620
And so these are the
disks where planets form,

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:55.912
and that's part of why it's
interesting because we want

00:04:55.912 --> 00:04:58.820
to look at how planets form.

00:04:58.820 --> 00:05:01.100
There's also outflows
as I'll show you.

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:03.370
And then, we'll get
these rings and gaps.

00:05:03.370 --> 00:05:06.220
And eventually, we'll
end up after maybe

00:05:06.220 --> 00:05:07.848
50 million years
or so, we end up

00:05:07.848 --> 00:05:10.390
with a system as a star in the
middle and a couple of planets

00:05:10.390 --> 00:05:11.000
around.

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:17.380
And so the phase I work on is
this phase from here over here

00:05:17.380 --> 00:05:18.400
too maybe here.

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:21.430
I'm looking at how this
disk and the stars interact.

00:05:23.950 --> 00:05:26.240
Why do we care?

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:27.652
Now, maybe you
think that's cool.

00:05:27.652 --> 00:05:29.860
And I think that's cool and
I think it's interesting.

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:32.470
But specifically, when I write
a funding proposal and say,

00:05:32.470 --> 00:05:35.000
dear NASA, please
give me $200,000

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:36.568
so I can keep working
on that, I need

00:05:36.568 --> 00:05:38.110
to have a slightly
better explanation

00:05:38.110 --> 00:05:39.790
than it's cool, right?

00:05:39.790 --> 00:05:42.760
So one of the things we
care for is the origin

00:05:42.760 --> 00:05:43.870
of planetary systems.

00:05:43.870 --> 00:05:46.150
How do planetary
systems form at all?

00:05:46.150 --> 00:05:47.740
How do you make planets?

00:05:47.740 --> 00:05:49.198
And in order to
understand that, we

00:05:49.198 --> 00:05:50.740
need to know what
the conditions are.

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:51.280
Is it hot?

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:51.970
Is it cold?

00:05:51.970 --> 00:05:52.990
It is dense?

00:05:52.990 --> 00:05:54.040
It is iron?

00:05:54.040 --> 00:05:55.460
Is there hydrogen?

00:05:55.460 --> 00:05:58.240
What actually is
the stuff made of?

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:01.240
We can also interpret
that as going back in time

00:06:01.240 --> 00:06:03.790
because we can't see
today how our Earth was

00:06:03.790 --> 00:06:05.920
formed because that was
5 billion years ago.

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:07.572
But we can take a
telescope and look

00:06:07.572 --> 00:06:09.280
at stellar systems
for Earth like planets

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:11.090
that are forming today.

00:06:11.090 --> 00:06:13.240
And so that looking
at those systems

00:06:13.240 --> 00:06:15.370
helps us understand what
the conditions were when

00:06:15.370 --> 00:06:18.880
the Earth was formed and how,
potentially, life on Earth

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:21.170
started and things like that.

00:06:21.170 --> 00:06:25.810
And one interesting
tidbit or aspect

00:06:25.810 --> 00:06:28.690
is that we think--
or one idea is

00:06:28.690 --> 00:06:31.810
that life, or at least
certain biomolecules,

00:06:31.810 --> 00:06:35.500
may have formed in
space first, and then

00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:38.860
come onto the Earth
frozen in as comets.

00:06:38.860 --> 00:06:43.210
Not necessarily-- don't
think of an animal walking,

00:06:43.210 --> 00:06:45.770
but think of amino acids.

00:06:45.770 --> 00:06:49.090
And we know that in these
large clouds of gas and dust

00:06:49.090 --> 00:06:51.670
using radio observations,
we can identify

00:06:51.670 --> 00:06:54.160
fairly complex molecules.

00:06:54.160 --> 00:06:57.280
Originally, people found carbon.

00:06:57.280 --> 00:06:58.660
Then they found carbon monoxide.

00:06:58.660 --> 00:07:00.260
Then they found carbon dioxide.

00:07:00.260 --> 00:07:03.050
But we now have-- we
found benzyl rings.

00:07:03.050 --> 00:07:06.580
So six carbon atoms in a
ring, the stuff around it.

00:07:06.580 --> 00:07:10.160
We found molecules that have
three benzyl rings on it.

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:11.680
So I don't know--

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:12.850
I don't work on radio.

00:07:12.850 --> 00:07:14.360
I don't know what
the latest one is.

00:07:14.360 --> 00:07:18.530
But the complexity of chemistry
in these clouds is astonishing.

00:07:18.530 --> 00:07:21.910
And so, almost certainly, some
of that rain down on the Earth.

00:07:21.910 --> 00:07:24.490
Is that important to
start life on a planet?

00:07:24.490 --> 00:07:25.363
I don't know.

00:07:25.363 --> 00:07:27.280
But it's certainly
interesting that it's there

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:28.773
and that the
building blocks don't

00:07:28.773 --> 00:07:30.190
actually-- or some
of the building

00:07:30.190 --> 00:07:34.473
blocks don't necessarily
have to form on a planet

00:07:34.473 --> 00:07:35.890
because they're
out there in space

00:07:35.890 --> 00:07:37.430
and they're just rained out.

00:07:37.430 --> 00:07:40.330
And so, again, for that-- to
understand if that's important,

00:07:40.330 --> 00:07:41.770
if that's cool,
if that's useful,

00:07:41.770 --> 00:07:43.270
if we want to know
a little bit more

00:07:43.270 --> 00:07:46.040
about the conditions of
how stars and planets form.

00:07:46.040 --> 00:07:47.150
So how do we do that?

00:07:47.150 --> 00:07:48.820
Well, we go and look.

00:07:48.820 --> 00:07:51.910
And when we go and look, we
have a choice in how we look.

00:07:51.910 --> 00:07:55.760
We have the entire what we
call electromagnetic spectrum.

00:07:55.760 --> 00:07:59.050
The thing that's most used
to us is visible light.

00:07:59.050 --> 00:08:00.760
That's what we
detect with our eyes.

00:08:00.760 --> 00:08:02.590
But there's a whole
range out there.

00:08:02.590 --> 00:08:04.870
And the visible light
is just this tiny bit

00:08:04.870 --> 00:08:07.600
of area of the spectrum.

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:09.490
It's just this tiny thing here.

00:08:09.490 --> 00:08:13.430
There's UV light, there's X-ray
light, there's gamma ray light,

00:08:13.430 --> 00:08:16.550
there's a huge range of infrared
light, there's microwaves,

00:08:16.550 --> 00:08:18.050
there's radio waves,
I already said.

00:08:18.050 --> 00:08:20.360
And radio, we can
identify these molecules.

00:08:20.360 --> 00:08:21.860
And by building the
right telescopes

00:08:21.860 --> 00:08:24.360
and the right instruments, we
can get a lot more information

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:27.140
than if we were just
looking in visible light.

00:08:27.140 --> 00:08:29.540
Here's an on Earth
demonstration of why

00:08:29.540 --> 00:08:31.160
that might be interesting.

00:08:31.160 --> 00:08:34.700
These are two animals, meerkats
and freshwater crocodiles,

00:08:34.700 --> 00:08:36.350
observed in two
different wavelengths.

00:08:36.350 --> 00:08:38.780
The top one is visible how
you would see it with your eye

00:08:38.780 --> 00:08:41.150
and the bottom one is
an infrared camera.

00:08:41.150 --> 00:08:43.850
And you see that although
they look fairly similar

00:08:43.850 --> 00:08:46.670
and have fairly similar
color, in visible light,

00:08:46.670 --> 00:08:48.590
they look vastly
different in the infrared.

00:08:48.590 --> 00:08:51.590
Now, I'm not a biologist, but
if you look closely enough,

00:08:51.590 --> 00:08:54.230
that tells you something about
the temperature of the animal

00:08:54.230 --> 00:08:57.080
and the skin conditions and
what the skin is made of

00:08:57.080 --> 00:08:59.040
and stuff like that if
you look closely enough.

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:00.507
And so we want to
use the analogy,

00:09:00.507 --> 00:09:02.090
we want to use the
same thing in space

00:09:02.090 --> 00:09:03.757
by looking at more
different wavelengths

00:09:03.757 --> 00:09:07.910
to probe more different
properties of these things.

00:09:07.910 --> 00:09:09.110
Slight wrinkle.

00:09:09.110 --> 00:09:12.110
Many of these wavelengths
don't make it to Earth.

00:09:12.110 --> 00:09:13.920
That's good because,
for example,

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:16.190
X-rays, if you have
too many X-rays,

00:09:16.190 --> 00:09:19.040
you're going to get cancer
if you get X-rays too much.

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:21.680
So fortunately, the
Earth's atmosphere

00:09:21.680 --> 00:09:25.300
blocks most of the X-rays
that come from space.

00:09:25.300 --> 00:09:26.957
And unfortunately,
for us, that means

00:09:26.957 --> 00:09:29.040
if we want to see the
X-rays that come from space,

00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:30.665
we have to build a
satellite and fly it

00:09:30.665 --> 00:09:32.870
above the Earth's atmosphere.

00:09:32.870 --> 00:09:35.450
That means it's expensive.

00:09:35.450 --> 00:09:37.610
The Chandra satellite
that I often work with

00:09:37.610 --> 00:09:40.460
or that I work for cost
about $1 to $2 billion,

00:09:40.460 --> 00:09:42.390
depending on how
exactly you count it.

00:09:42.390 --> 00:09:44.150
And if something is
broken, it's gone.

00:09:44.150 --> 00:09:44.790
It's in space.

00:09:44.790 --> 00:09:46.700
You can't just
fly up and fix it.

00:09:46.700 --> 00:09:48.680
For the Hubble Space
Telescope, where

00:09:48.680 --> 00:09:50.720
they went up with
space shuttles to fix

00:09:50.720 --> 00:09:54.010
things, every time you go up and
fix something, it's $1 billion.

00:09:54.010 --> 00:09:55.220
So it's not like--

00:09:55.220 --> 00:09:58.390
you can't just go and tighten
the screw if you need to.

00:09:58.390 --> 00:10:01.080
So if you can observe it
from Earth, that's great.

00:10:01.080 --> 00:10:04.380
But certain things we just can't
because there's air in between.

00:10:04.380 --> 00:10:07.010
And so, not incidentally,
the wavelengths

00:10:07.010 --> 00:10:10.890
that make it to Earth
best is the visible light,

00:10:10.890 --> 00:10:13.010
which is why our eyes see
visible light because we

00:10:13.010 --> 00:10:14.130
have plenty of that there.

00:10:14.130 --> 00:10:15.680
But if you want to
observe in several

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:18.505
of these other wavebands, we
have to observe satellites--

00:10:18.505 --> 00:10:19.130
near satellite.

00:10:19.130 --> 00:10:21.770
So in X-rays, we can use,
for example, Chandra.

00:10:21.770 --> 00:10:24.890
There's a second one, a
European one called XMM-Newton.

00:10:24.890 --> 00:10:27.230
In the UV, the Hubble
Space Telescope

00:10:27.230 --> 00:10:28.220
is still the best one.

00:10:28.220 --> 00:10:30.440
And this is a picture of
the James Webb Telescope

00:10:30.440 --> 00:10:33.470
that observes in the
infrared recently seen.

00:10:33.470 --> 00:10:36.110
So there are solar panels
that power Chandra.

00:10:36.110 --> 00:10:37.850
The X-rays go in here.

00:10:37.850 --> 00:10:39.230
Chandra doesn't make X-rays.

00:10:39.230 --> 00:10:40.400
It's not an X-ray machine.

00:10:40.400 --> 00:10:41.180
It's a telescope.

00:10:41.180 --> 00:10:44.510
Observes X-rays that
come from space anyway.

00:10:44.510 --> 00:10:46.790
The sun makes X-rays,
black holes make X-rays,

00:10:46.790 --> 00:10:49.410
and we just need to see them.

00:10:49.410 --> 00:10:50.480
And so it's very long.

00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:51.980
Chandra was launched
with the space shuttle.

00:10:51.980 --> 00:10:54.438
It's actually the heaviest
thing the space shuttle has ever

00:10:54.438 --> 00:10:56.450
launched.

00:10:56.450 --> 00:10:59.722
And the detectors
are here in the back.

00:10:59.722 --> 00:11:01.430
And I can say a little
bit more about how

00:11:01.430 --> 00:11:02.900
Chandra is made from--

00:11:02.900 --> 00:11:05.540
or how it was made
later if we get to it.

00:11:05.540 --> 00:11:07.790
OK, I said we want to observe
in different wavelengths

00:11:07.790 --> 00:11:09.582
because they give us
different information.

00:11:09.582 --> 00:11:11.570
This is a star forming region.

00:11:11.570 --> 00:11:13.850
That's what people call
the Pillars of Creation

00:11:13.850 --> 00:11:17.270
in the Eagle Nebula observed
in different wavelengths.

00:11:17.270 --> 00:11:20.330
On the left is visible
light observed with Hubble.

00:11:20.330 --> 00:11:22.160
This is near-infrared light.

00:11:22.160 --> 00:11:25.230
And then, on the far side
is this mid-infrared light,

00:11:25.230 --> 00:11:26.480
so even longer wavelengths.

00:11:26.480 --> 00:11:28.940
And you see that although
the exact same region,

00:11:28.940 --> 00:11:32.360
you see different things.

00:11:32.360 --> 00:11:35.390
For example, when you look
in the optical with Hubble,

00:11:35.390 --> 00:11:38.700
you see these dense
clouds of gas and dust,

00:11:38.700 --> 00:11:40.407
where I told you stars form.

00:11:40.407 --> 00:11:42.490
I told you stars last form,
but you don't actually

00:11:42.490 --> 00:11:45.400
see that because all
hidden behind that cloud.

00:11:45.400 --> 00:11:46.870
When you look in
the mid-infrared,

00:11:46.870 --> 00:11:48.820
that dust is transparent.

00:11:48.820 --> 00:11:51.170
And because it's transparent,
or mostly transparent,

00:11:51.170 --> 00:11:53.650
you can see all these stars
here and you can actually

00:11:53.650 --> 00:11:56.440
go up and count them.

00:11:56.440 --> 00:11:59.070
And if you go into
the mid-infrared,

00:11:59.070 --> 00:12:02.980
then their dust isn't black,
it's actually glowing.

00:12:02.980 --> 00:12:04.570
And because it's
glowing, we can now

00:12:04.570 --> 00:12:06.490
measure the properties
of the dust.

00:12:06.490 --> 00:12:08.680
We can look at the light
that comes from the dust.

00:12:08.680 --> 00:12:11.140
We can see how much it
glows or what color it has

00:12:11.140 --> 00:12:12.670
and that tells us
what temperature

00:12:12.670 --> 00:12:15.128
it has, for example, and that's
how we know how cold it is.

00:12:15.128 --> 00:12:16.948
I said it's about
100 Kelvin or so.

00:12:16.948 --> 00:12:17.740
That's how we know.

00:12:17.740 --> 00:12:19.960
And that's how we know
what it's made of.

00:12:19.960 --> 00:12:21.340
Yes.

00:12:21.340 --> 00:12:26.770
How do you know that a star is
in the cloud and not behind it?

00:12:26.770 --> 00:12:28.540
That's a very good question.

00:12:28.540 --> 00:12:31.060
There's several
things we can do.

00:12:31.060 --> 00:12:36.280
The easiest is this--

00:12:36.280 --> 00:12:37.780
so there is a complex answer.

00:12:37.780 --> 00:12:39.430
And not every
technique will work

00:12:39.430 --> 00:12:40.690
for every one of those stars.

00:12:40.690 --> 00:12:42.910
One of the best things or
easiest things to measure

00:12:42.910 --> 00:12:46.210
distances is you
measure-- you point--

00:12:46.210 --> 00:12:48.310
you look at it from
here and then you

00:12:48.310 --> 00:12:51.400
wait six months until the Earth
is on the other side of the sun

00:12:51.400 --> 00:12:53.470
and measure it from
here and there'll

00:12:53.470 --> 00:12:55.570
be a difference in angle.

00:12:55.570 --> 00:12:56.500
It's called parallax.

00:12:56.500 --> 00:12:57.875
And that difference
in angle, you

00:12:57.875 --> 00:13:00.760
can use a sign and
elementary geometry

00:13:00.760 --> 00:13:02.140
and transform that
to a distance.

00:13:02.140 --> 00:13:04.850
So that's one way, for example.

00:13:04.850 --> 00:13:08.590
In this particular case,
that's not terribly useful

00:13:08.590 --> 00:13:12.290
because it's not that precise
and the Nebula isn't that deep.

00:13:12.290 --> 00:13:15.970
But if you can identify
the same star in,

00:13:15.970 --> 00:13:19.040
for example, in this
image and in this image,

00:13:19.040 --> 00:13:22.300
then you can say, for
example, this one,

00:13:22.300 --> 00:13:26.380
you could say there's
lots of stuff behind it,

00:13:26.380 --> 00:13:27.580
lots of Nebula.

00:13:27.580 --> 00:13:29.530
I can't see anything
else behind it.

00:13:29.530 --> 00:13:32.810
So it's clearly in front
of this dense region.

00:13:32.810 --> 00:13:35.770
And yet, I can't see it here.

00:13:35.770 --> 00:13:38.510
So there must also be still
some material in front of it,

00:13:38.510 --> 00:13:40.300
so it's probably in the middle.

00:13:40.300 --> 00:13:42.310
That doesn't give me
exactly where it is

00:13:42.310 --> 00:13:44.650
and it doesn't give me a 3D map.

00:13:44.650 --> 00:13:47.410
But it tells me it's
not far-- at least,

00:13:47.410 --> 00:13:48.700
it's not very far behind it.

00:13:48.700 --> 00:13:50.140
It's not very far
in front of it.

00:13:50.140 --> 00:13:53.050
It's associated with that cloud.

00:13:53.050 --> 00:13:55.720
In other clusters that are
further-- that are closer by,

00:13:55.720 --> 00:13:58.330
what you can also do is you
can look at how stars move.

00:13:58.330 --> 00:13:59.980
Because the galaxy rotates.

00:13:59.980 --> 00:14:01.640
Stuff moves around.

00:14:01.640 --> 00:14:05.110
And so when you have one of
these clouds and it moves,

00:14:05.110 --> 00:14:08.290
then all the objects in it
should move the same way

00:14:08.290 --> 00:14:10.030
on the sky.

00:14:10.030 --> 00:14:12.700
And things that are far away on
the other end of the galaxies

00:14:12.700 --> 00:14:14.560
aren't going to move
in the same direction.

00:14:14.560 --> 00:14:16.180
We call that proper motion.

00:14:16.180 --> 00:14:18.940
Now, of course, stars
don't move terribly fast.

00:14:18.940 --> 00:14:20.320
I mean, if you
look out at night,

00:14:20.320 --> 00:14:24.100
if you see something
move, it's not a star.

00:14:24.100 --> 00:14:27.970
A typical speed would be--

00:14:27.970 --> 00:14:29.890
so we take a degree, right?

00:14:29.890 --> 00:14:33.910
One degree of distance is 1
degree is about this much.

00:14:33.910 --> 00:14:36.610
A degree consists
of 60 arc minutes.

00:14:36.610 --> 00:14:40.030
Each arc minutes consists
of 60 arc seconds.

00:14:40.030 --> 00:14:41.830
And now, I can't do
that with my fingers

00:14:41.830 --> 00:14:43.540
because I can't
make it that small.

00:14:43.540 --> 00:14:45.170
And then, we need it
smaller than that.

00:14:45.170 --> 00:14:51.340
So every arc second we pick
down in 1,000 milliarcseconds.

00:14:51.340 --> 00:14:55.630
And these stars may move a
tenth of a milliarcsecond a year

00:14:55.630 --> 00:14:56.540
or so.

00:14:56.540 --> 00:14:59.410
So you do need some big
telescopes and fairly precise

00:14:59.410 --> 00:15:00.057
measurements.

00:15:00.057 --> 00:15:02.140
But if you can make that
and their satellites that

00:15:02.140 --> 00:15:04.000
are purpose built
for just that purpose

00:15:04.000 --> 00:15:06.250
and you see that all the
stuff moves together,

00:15:06.250 --> 00:15:08.757
then they're probably part
of the same association.

00:15:11.740 --> 00:15:14.170
I'll talk a lot about
the Orion Nebula cloud.

00:15:14.170 --> 00:15:16.720
I already told you some of
the things of the Orion Nebula

00:15:16.720 --> 00:15:17.220
cloud.

00:15:17.220 --> 00:15:19.090
So I at least want to
tell you where it is.

00:15:19.090 --> 00:15:21.880
Orion is probably
one of the few things

00:15:21.880 --> 00:15:23.200
most people can see on the sky.

00:15:23.200 --> 00:15:24.880
And even in Boston
you can find Orion

00:15:24.880 --> 00:15:26.500
because it's one of the
few that are bright enough

00:15:26.500 --> 00:15:27.520
that you can see that.

00:15:27.520 --> 00:15:30.010
I know about six constellations
because I find that most

00:15:30.010 --> 00:15:31.037
people I talk to three.

00:15:31.037 --> 00:15:32.870
So if you know six,
that makes you an expert

00:15:32.870 --> 00:15:33.790
and that's good enough
and I don't have

00:15:33.790 --> 00:15:34.957
to remember anymore of that.

00:15:34.957 --> 00:15:38.410
But Orion has this popular
belt of three stars next to it.

00:15:38.410 --> 00:15:40.510
And then, you have the
feet and the hands.

00:15:40.510 --> 00:15:42.423
And just down here
is Orion's sword.

00:15:42.423 --> 00:15:43.840
And in the middle
of Orion's sword

00:15:43.840 --> 00:15:47.590
is this Orion Nebula, this Orion
Nebula that I talked before.

00:15:47.590 --> 00:15:50.140
So the Orion Nebula is
actually bright enough

00:15:50.140 --> 00:15:53.210
that you can see it visible
by eye if you are, let's say,

00:15:53.210 --> 00:15:56.380
in Maine or Vermont or the
Mojave desert or somewhere

00:15:56.380 --> 00:15:57.250
it's actually dark.

00:15:57.250 --> 00:15:58.240
Don't try in Boston.

00:15:58.240 --> 00:15:59.440
Pointless.

00:15:59.440 --> 00:16:02.260
But in dark spaces,
you can see it.

00:16:02.260 --> 00:16:04.430
OK, so I already showed
you a couple of images.

00:16:04.430 --> 00:16:06.597
And one of the techniques
we learn about young stars

00:16:06.597 --> 00:16:07.880
is we do images of them.

00:16:07.880 --> 00:16:12.830
So here's real images from
the Orion Nebula cloud.

00:16:12.830 --> 00:16:15.570
I told you that images-- that
stars have disk around them.

00:16:15.570 --> 00:16:16.590
How do we know that?

00:16:16.590 --> 00:16:19.520
Well, here's an image
that looks a little bit

00:16:19.520 --> 00:16:21.830
like a black saucer or cigar.

00:16:21.830 --> 00:16:25.490
What that is is the Nebula
behind it is glowing.

00:16:25.490 --> 00:16:29.480
And then, we are looking at
the edge of one of those disks.

00:16:29.480 --> 00:16:31.220
And the disk is
colder and denser

00:16:31.220 --> 00:16:34.440
than the rest of the Nebula,
so it makes a black shadow.

00:16:34.440 --> 00:16:37.940
And so now we can go and we know
how far away Orion is and we

00:16:37.940 --> 00:16:39.620
can measure the size of this.

00:16:39.620 --> 00:16:41.810
And then, we can
calculate the size

00:16:41.810 --> 00:16:43.970
in kilometers of whatever
unit you want to use.

00:16:43.970 --> 00:16:46.250
Astronomers often use
centimeters, although numbers

00:16:46.250 --> 00:16:47.390
are really high.

00:16:47.390 --> 00:16:49.580
We can calculate the size
in centimeters how long

00:16:49.580 --> 00:16:51.400
and how thick that is.

00:16:51.400 --> 00:16:52.930
Why is this Nebula glowing?

00:16:52.930 --> 00:16:55.090
Why is the Orion
Nebula glowing at all?

00:16:55.090 --> 00:16:57.640
It's like driving
your car through fog.

00:16:57.640 --> 00:16:59.320
It doesn't glow by itself.

00:16:59.320 --> 00:17:02.590
There's bright stars that
shine light on the Nebula

00:17:02.590 --> 00:17:04.490
and it reflects it to us.

00:17:04.490 --> 00:17:06.190
So it's a reflection Nebula.

00:17:06.190 --> 00:17:08.079
Here's another one.

00:17:08.079 --> 00:17:09.790
And what do you see?

00:17:09.790 --> 00:17:13.069
The glowing stuff here in the
middle, that's not the star.

00:17:13.069 --> 00:17:13.900
The star is tiny.

00:17:13.900 --> 00:17:16.690
The star is tinier than
the tip of the mouse.

00:17:16.690 --> 00:17:19.329
But what happens is
that, around this disk,

00:17:19.329 --> 00:17:21.369
there's other gas and dust.

00:17:21.369 --> 00:17:23.380
The star can't shines
through the disk

00:17:23.380 --> 00:17:24.829
because the disk is too thick.

00:17:24.829 --> 00:17:27.230
But it shines upward
and stuff gets reflected

00:17:27.230 --> 00:17:29.160
and we see the reflected light.

00:17:29.160 --> 00:17:32.730
Even more obvious here, the star
itself sits here in the middle

00:17:32.730 --> 00:17:33.890
here.

00:17:33.890 --> 00:17:37.760
This is the black
disk left and right.

00:17:37.760 --> 00:17:41.920
It goes kind of diagonally
that we look at from the side.

00:17:41.920 --> 00:17:44.820
And then, this is
all gas flowing out

00:17:44.820 --> 00:17:47.700
from the star that
reflects the starlight.

00:17:47.700 --> 00:17:50.200
And so we can see now
which direction it goes.

00:17:50.200 --> 00:17:53.040
We can see that it's less
here, which is why it's black.

00:17:53.040 --> 00:17:54.060
Nothing reflects here.

00:17:54.060 --> 00:17:56.610
There's a little more here
we can learn about how

00:17:56.610 --> 00:17:59.390
gas flows out from stars.

00:17:59.390 --> 00:18:00.860
Here's another thing.

00:18:00.860 --> 00:18:03.950
Here, the star sits
here and it has a disk.

00:18:03.950 --> 00:18:07.310
And what you can't see because
there's a cutout, way over here

00:18:07.310 --> 00:18:08.690
is a very bright star.

00:18:08.690 --> 00:18:12.980
This very bright star shines
on that disk and evaporates it.

00:18:12.980 --> 00:18:16.220
It's so bright that it,
essentially, boils off material

00:18:16.220 --> 00:18:18.200
and then reflects and shines.

00:18:18.200 --> 00:18:20.960
And so what you see here is a
reflection from this material

00:18:20.960 --> 00:18:22.610
that boils off the disk.

00:18:22.610 --> 00:18:23.940
Why do we care?

00:18:23.940 --> 00:18:25.820
Well, if you want to
know how planets form,

00:18:25.820 --> 00:18:29.600
we need to know how long
these disks survive.

00:18:29.600 --> 00:18:31.550
Do you have a million
years to make a planet?

00:18:31.550 --> 00:18:33.342
Do you have 100,000
years to make a planet?

00:18:33.342 --> 00:18:35.520
Do you have 10 million
years to have a planet?

00:18:35.520 --> 00:18:38.090
And so one thing that
matters is, is there

00:18:38.090 --> 00:18:41.760
a bright star nearby that
boils off all the material?

00:18:41.760 --> 00:18:44.870
And so we measure
the size of the disk

00:18:44.870 --> 00:18:46.838
and calculate the mass
of a disk when there's

00:18:46.838 --> 00:18:48.380
a bright star nearby
and when there's

00:18:48.380 --> 00:18:49.610
not a bright star nearby.

00:18:49.610 --> 00:18:52.730
And we can say, OK, bright stars
are bad for making planets,

00:18:52.730 --> 00:18:54.350
at least when
you're close enough.

00:18:54.350 --> 00:18:55.712
And now, I'm saying bad.

00:18:55.712 --> 00:18:57.920
But of course, you could
imagine working out a number

00:18:57.920 --> 00:19:00.378
and saying if the star is this
bright and I'm this far away

00:19:00.378 --> 00:19:03.358
then it's going to evaporate
X kilograms per second.

00:19:03.358 --> 00:19:05.150
I don't have that number
on top of my head.

00:19:05.150 --> 00:19:07.550
I'm not going to bore you
with the exact number anyway.

00:19:07.550 --> 00:19:08.960
But you could calculate that.

00:19:08.960 --> 00:19:09.583
Yes.

00:19:09.583 --> 00:19:11.750
In that picture up there,
that central part you said

00:19:11.750 --> 00:19:13.210
is the disk, right?

00:19:13.210 --> 00:19:15.830
Either side of the disk,
is that the young star

00:19:15.830 --> 00:19:17.690
forming or two other stars?

00:19:17.690 --> 00:19:18.560
Here?

00:19:18.560 --> 00:19:20.460
On either side
the bright lights.

00:19:20.460 --> 00:19:22.340
Those are not-- the
star itself, there's

00:19:22.340 --> 00:19:25.760
one star forming
here in the middle.

00:19:25.760 --> 00:19:28.160
But you don't see it because
it's behind the disk.

00:19:28.160 --> 00:19:29.740
Oh, it's behind the disk.

00:19:29.740 --> 00:19:33.540
So if the disk looks
like this, right?

00:19:33.540 --> 00:19:36.463
You're looking at the edge of
the disk and the stars here.

00:19:36.463 --> 00:19:37.380
The tip of my fingers.

00:19:37.380 --> 00:19:38.260
You don't see it.

00:19:38.260 --> 00:19:41.070
But there's stuff
going in and out.

00:19:41.070 --> 00:19:44.140
There's gas and dust on the
top and the bottom of that.

00:19:44.140 --> 00:19:46.620
And so the star
shines on that dust

00:19:46.620 --> 00:19:48.120
and it reflects back to you.

00:19:48.120 --> 00:19:50.810
Imagine me taking a flashlight
and putting it in the fog

00:19:50.810 --> 00:19:51.310
up here.

00:19:51.310 --> 00:19:53.018
You're going to see
the fog or the Nebula

00:19:53.018 --> 00:19:55.950
up and down below even though
there is no star because star

00:19:55.950 --> 00:19:57.420
shines in it from the bottom.

00:19:57.420 --> 00:19:59.050
The disk has a
hole in the middle.

00:19:59.050 --> 00:20:01.710
And so the light can't make
it through this thick layer

00:20:01.710 --> 00:20:03.930
of disk, but it can make it up.

00:20:03.930 --> 00:20:06.390
And then, if something
is here that reflects it,

00:20:06.390 --> 00:20:08.160
let's say, a piece
of ice, then it

00:20:08.160 --> 00:20:10.260
goes in the direction
you can see it.

00:20:10.260 --> 00:20:13.140
Is the disk itself
irrelevant to the formation

00:20:13.140 --> 00:20:15.410
of the young star?

00:20:15.410 --> 00:20:16.800
That's what I'm working on.

00:20:16.800 --> 00:20:18.870
So yes it is.

00:20:18.870 --> 00:20:19.370
I apologize.

00:20:19.370 --> 00:20:21.110
No, no, no need to
apologize, right?

00:20:21.110 --> 00:20:21.650
Yes it is.

00:20:21.650 --> 00:20:22.710
That's a reasonable question.

00:20:22.710 --> 00:20:24.800
That's a question you should
totally write in as a proposal

00:20:24.800 --> 00:20:25.550
and find out.

00:20:25.550 --> 00:20:27.480
Now, we know some
about it already.

00:20:27.480 --> 00:20:30.300
So we probably have to say a
little more than let's do that.

00:20:30.300 --> 00:20:36.050
But at this stage, most of the
mass is already in the star.

00:20:36.050 --> 00:20:38.840
But if you look at
our Earth today,

00:20:38.840 --> 00:20:41.100
there's only planets left
and the disk is gone.

00:20:41.100 --> 00:20:43.130
And so we can
measure or calculate

00:20:43.130 --> 00:20:46.070
how massive is that
disk and what mass

00:20:46.070 --> 00:20:47.540
do we have in our planets.

00:20:47.540 --> 00:20:50.450
Just take the mass of Mars and
Earth and Jupiter and Saturn

00:20:50.450 --> 00:20:51.260
and add them up.

00:20:51.260 --> 00:20:54.740
And there's only about 1% of
the mass of an average disk.

00:20:54.740 --> 00:20:56.270
So where is the
rest of the mass go?

00:20:56.270 --> 00:20:58.910
Well, some of it falls into
the star and some of it

00:20:58.910 --> 00:21:00.290
evaporates off.

00:21:00.290 --> 00:21:01.880
And what fraction goes where?

00:21:01.880 --> 00:21:04.580
Well, that's what we
try to measure, right?

00:21:04.580 --> 00:21:07.820
And that influences
the star in some way.

00:21:07.820 --> 00:21:11.330
How exactly, it depends
a little bit on the star.

00:21:11.330 --> 00:21:13.970
It could change, for example,
the chemical composition

00:21:13.970 --> 00:21:18.920
because these disks that
are made of ice and dust.

00:21:18.920 --> 00:21:21.320
Does the gas, like
helium and hydrogen,

00:21:21.320 --> 00:21:24.590
does it evaporate off first and
all the metals clump together

00:21:24.590 --> 00:21:25.670
and make planets?

00:21:25.670 --> 00:21:27.920
Do all the metals
then fold in the star?

00:21:27.920 --> 00:21:29.990
Do mostly the gas
falls into the star?

00:21:29.990 --> 00:21:31.610
That's what we want to measure.

00:21:34.980 --> 00:21:36.360
OK, here's a couple more images.

00:21:36.360 --> 00:21:37.860
Here is images of jets.

00:21:37.860 --> 00:21:39.750
And I already talked
about proper motion

00:21:39.750 --> 00:21:42.100
where you see things
move on the sky.

00:21:42.100 --> 00:21:45.720
This is a star-- famous
star called HH30.

00:21:45.720 --> 00:21:48.520
And the stars in the middle and
you see the disk on the side

00:21:48.520 --> 00:21:49.020
again.

00:21:49.020 --> 00:21:50.760
And then, you have this jet--

00:21:50.760 --> 00:21:54.930
this really collimated stream
of stuff going up and down.

00:21:54.930 --> 00:21:56.910
And here, we can
measure how fast

00:21:56.910 --> 00:21:59.760
it's going out from the star
because we have pictures

00:21:59.760 --> 00:22:02.640
from 1995, 1998, and 2000.

00:22:02.640 --> 00:22:06.300
And so you can take this
dot and measure how far

00:22:06.300 --> 00:22:07.830
is this dot from the star.

00:22:07.830 --> 00:22:10.440
And then, you can look at the
same dot three years later

00:22:10.440 --> 00:22:12.630
and see how far away
from the star is it now

00:22:12.630 --> 00:22:15.950
and how far away from
the star is it now.

00:22:15.950 --> 00:22:18.708
And if you know how fast
the way that thing is,

00:22:18.708 --> 00:22:21.250
how far away it is, then you
can calculate how fast it moves,

00:22:21.250 --> 00:22:24.640
and it's around 300
miles per second.

00:22:24.640 --> 00:22:27.188
And that tells us, well,
we can take the star

00:22:27.188 --> 00:22:28.980
and we can look at the
gravitational energy

00:22:28.980 --> 00:22:31.290
and say, where
does it come from?

00:22:31.290 --> 00:22:33.505
This jet is not
formed in the disk

00:22:33.505 --> 00:22:35.130
because disk doesn't
have enough energy

00:22:35.130 --> 00:22:36.580
to make anything that fast.

00:22:36.580 --> 00:22:39.780
So this jet must be coming from
very, very close to the star.

00:22:39.780 --> 00:22:42.060
And so the material that's
thrown out of the jet

00:22:42.060 --> 00:22:45.110
is stuff that's from
the star, or that's,

00:22:45.110 --> 00:22:46.860
at least, from the
inner edge of the disk.

00:22:46.860 --> 00:22:49.360
It's not something further out.

00:22:49.360 --> 00:22:52.690
Now, I'm not going to give
you the answer to everything

00:22:52.690 --> 00:22:55.190
because, first of
all, I don't know it.

00:22:55.190 --> 00:22:56.650
We also still do
research on that.

00:22:56.650 --> 00:22:58.150
And there's also
there's literally

00:22:58.150 --> 00:22:59.840
hundreds of people
researching that

00:22:59.840 --> 00:23:02.132
and I can't tell you everything
that hundreds of people

00:23:02.132 --> 00:23:05.110
have spent the last 50 years
working on in 30 minutes.

00:23:05.110 --> 00:23:07.210
But that gives us
information about where

00:23:07.210 --> 00:23:10.240
that material comes from.

00:23:10.240 --> 00:23:14.010
Now, this is a jet
that I've looked at.

00:23:14.010 --> 00:23:15.750
And this is one of
those cases where

00:23:15.750 --> 00:23:18.700
I said images don't
necessarily look good.

00:23:18.700 --> 00:23:21.690
So this is observed with
the Chandra X-ray telescope.

00:23:21.690 --> 00:23:26.790
And every dot-- every gray dot
here-- every light gray dot is

00:23:26.790 --> 00:23:29.940
one photon, one piece of light.

00:23:29.940 --> 00:23:31.800
Every dark gray dot
is two pieces of light

00:23:31.800 --> 00:23:33.720
and every black one is
three pieces of light.

00:23:36.810 --> 00:23:40.590
Each of those images is about
a day of looking at that star.

00:23:40.590 --> 00:23:42.120
You don't see the star itself.

00:23:42.120 --> 00:23:44.610
The star is with
these little X's are.

00:23:44.610 --> 00:23:47.877
Or you barely see that's the
star itself because, again,

00:23:47.877 --> 00:23:49.710
it's one of those cases
where we are looking

00:23:49.710 --> 00:23:51.420
through the disk from the side.

00:23:51.420 --> 00:23:54.420
And then, we are interested
in the jet that comes out.

00:23:54.420 --> 00:23:55.980
I showed you that
picture before how

00:23:55.980 --> 00:23:58.950
the jet moves-- how this
jet moves in the optical

00:23:58.950 --> 00:23:59.945
observed as Hubble.

00:23:59.945 --> 00:24:01.320
We would like to
know of the jets

00:24:01.320 --> 00:24:02.653
that we see in X-rays also move.

00:24:02.653 --> 00:24:04.195
So first of all, we
need to find one.

00:24:04.195 --> 00:24:06.540
And then, we need to find
a feature that moves.

00:24:06.540 --> 00:24:09.310
Look in this gray box here.

00:24:09.310 --> 00:24:13.090
In 2001, that's
one piece of light.

00:24:13.090 --> 00:24:19.060
In 2005, there's 1, 2, 3, 4, 5,
6, 7 photons pieces of light.

00:24:19.060 --> 00:24:21.790
And then, in 2009,
there's only two.

00:24:21.790 --> 00:24:23.800
So I think that means
that it's material

00:24:23.800 --> 00:24:27.010
that came from the star that
wasn't there in 2005 that moved

00:24:27.010 --> 00:24:29.560
into that position in 2009.

00:24:29.560 --> 00:24:33.770
And then, it cooled off and
so it moved out and cooled off

00:24:33.770 --> 00:24:36.950
so we can't see it anymore.

00:24:36.950 --> 00:24:42.790
Now, that's from two and seven
photons and pieces of light.

00:24:42.790 --> 00:24:44.780
So we try our best.

00:24:44.780 --> 00:24:47.140
But if you want to
make a pretty picture

00:24:47.140 --> 00:24:50.920
in multiple different
images, go ahead, right?

00:24:50.920 --> 00:24:52.360
It's going to be hard.

00:24:52.360 --> 00:24:55.450
But that's why depending on what
research you do and what you

00:24:55.450 --> 00:24:57.920
look at, sometimes it looks
good and sometimes it doesn't.

00:24:57.920 --> 00:25:01.420
So what we find is from stuff
like this that things that--

00:25:01.420 --> 00:25:04.450
the jets that shine in
X-rays are faster than

00:25:04.450 --> 00:25:06.640
the ones that shine
in the optical.

00:25:06.640 --> 00:25:10.000
And what we think that
means is that jets are not

00:25:10.000 --> 00:25:11.740
like a homogeneous stream.

00:25:11.740 --> 00:25:14.620
It's actually layers of streams,
like the layers of an onion.

00:25:14.620 --> 00:25:17.530
There's something in the
middle that's faster.

00:25:17.530 --> 00:25:20.320
And then, there's an outer
layer that's a little slower.

00:25:20.320 --> 00:25:22.510
And a layer further out
that's a little slower

00:25:22.510 --> 00:25:25.150
and a layer further out that's
a little slower than that

00:25:25.150 --> 00:25:31.140
until you get a very slow
motion out to where the disk is.

00:25:31.140 --> 00:25:33.368
And again, that tells us
where it is coming from.

00:25:33.368 --> 00:25:34.410
Is it coming from a star?

00:25:34.410 --> 00:25:35.550
Is it coming from the disk?

00:25:35.550 --> 00:25:38.770
We can measure those things.

00:25:38.770 --> 00:25:40.408
OK, back to the
Orion Nebula cloud.

00:25:40.408 --> 00:25:42.700
I think I've showed that
picture a couple of times now.

00:25:42.700 --> 00:25:45.980
In different images, I've
talked about all the pieces.

00:25:45.980 --> 00:25:48.920
Here's an X-ray movie.

00:25:48.920 --> 00:25:52.430
So this is a month of looking
at the Orion Nebula cloud.

00:25:52.430 --> 00:25:55.520
We see that it's twinkling
and you see that some of them

00:25:55.520 --> 00:25:56.660
change colors.

00:25:56.660 --> 00:25:59.630
Every one of these
dots is a star.

00:25:59.630 --> 00:26:04.110
And you see that they're
becoming brighter and fainter.

00:26:04.110 --> 00:26:07.170
I should first say, when
you look with your eye

00:26:07.170 --> 00:26:11.230
and you see stars twinkling,
that doesn't mean anything.

00:26:11.230 --> 00:26:14.490
It's because the Earth's
atmosphere is in the way.

00:26:14.490 --> 00:26:16.470
If you were to take
your eyes into space

00:26:16.470 --> 00:26:18.330
and look straight
into space, well, OK,

00:26:18.330 --> 00:26:20.200
you'll die because
you need to breathe.

00:26:20.200 --> 00:26:22.860
But apart from that,
stars wouldn't--

00:26:22.860 --> 00:26:26.670
with very, very few exceptions,
they wouldn't twinkle.

00:26:26.670 --> 00:26:30.060
The twinkling is the
motion of gas between us

00:26:30.060 --> 00:26:32.610
and the Earth's atmosphere
as wind and high layers

00:26:32.610 --> 00:26:35.010
that refract the
light and break it.

00:26:35.010 --> 00:26:36.960
So it's actually
very annoying when

00:26:36.960 --> 00:26:38.640
you do telescope
observations on ground.

00:26:38.640 --> 00:26:40.120
You have to try to correct that.

00:26:40.120 --> 00:26:44.550
But in X-rays, stars
become brighter and fainter

00:26:44.550 --> 00:26:45.600
significantly.

00:26:45.600 --> 00:26:49.640
Like sometimes, they
become 20 times brighter.

00:26:49.640 --> 00:26:51.280
And so you see
things appearing here

00:26:51.280 --> 00:26:53.530
that weren't even there before.

00:26:53.530 --> 00:26:55.030
We can show that
in a different way.

00:26:55.030 --> 00:26:58.110
And that different way is
what we call a light curve.

00:26:58.110 --> 00:27:01.160
So what we do is we have
the time at the bottom.

00:27:01.160 --> 00:27:03.705
In this case, in a unit
called kiloseconds, right?

00:27:03.705 --> 00:27:04.830
Because that's what you do.

00:27:04.830 --> 00:27:07.340
You do meters, centimeters,
and kilometers.

00:27:07.340 --> 00:27:09.870
That would be clearly
be grams and kilograms.

00:27:09.870 --> 00:27:12.200
So that would be
seconds and kiloseconds.

00:27:12.200 --> 00:27:15.010
But whatever.

00:27:15.010 --> 00:27:17.610
And so we measure how bright
it is at this time when

00:27:17.610 --> 00:27:18.540
we start measuring.

00:27:18.540 --> 00:27:20.280
And then, we measure how
bright it's here and how bright

00:27:20.280 --> 00:27:21.120
it's here and how
bright it's here

00:27:21.120 --> 00:27:22.662
and we measure how
bright it's there.

00:27:22.662 --> 00:27:24.660
And it suddenly
becomes a lot brighter.

00:27:24.660 --> 00:27:26.340
And we call that
a stellar flare.

00:27:26.340 --> 00:27:27.900
We see this in
X-rays, and sometimes,

00:27:27.900 --> 00:27:29.520
we see it in the optical,
although in the optical

00:27:29.520 --> 00:27:31.770
they're not as strong, you
wouldn't-- they're so weak,

00:27:31.770 --> 00:27:34.948
you wouldn't see
them with your eyes.

00:27:34.948 --> 00:27:36.240
How do we know what that means?

00:27:36.240 --> 00:27:38.850
Well, it'd be great if
there was a star nearby

00:27:38.850 --> 00:27:41.580
where we could see what
those things actually mean.

00:27:41.580 --> 00:27:44.970
Turns out there is one, and we
see it every day, it's the sun.

00:27:44.970 --> 00:27:49.190
So here's a similar
event on the sun.

00:27:49.190 --> 00:27:51.950
This is taken with a
satellite called SDO.

00:27:54.990 --> 00:27:57.990
And you see these
arc loops here.

00:27:57.990 --> 00:28:00.240
Now, I've picked one where
these arc loops happen

00:28:00.240 --> 00:28:02.280
to be on the side of
the sun because then you

00:28:02.280 --> 00:28:04.405
can see it much better than
when it's in the middle

00:28:04.405 --> 00:28:05.880
and the sun is behind.

00:28:05.880 --> 00:28:08.220
These are magnetic field lines.

00:28:08.220 --> 00:28:09.718
The sun has a magnetic field.

00:28:09.718 --> 00:28:11.760
And there is, essentially,
a North Pole over here

00:28:11.760 --> 00:28:13.590
and a South Pole over there.

00:28:13.590 --> 00:28:15.870
And then, along these
magnetic field lines,

00:28:15.870 --> 00:28:17.130
the gas can heat up.

00:28:17.130 --> 00:28:20.970
And when it heats up, this is a
movie taken in the ultraviolet,

00:28:20.970 --> 00:28:24.160
then becomes hot enough to
shine in the ultraviolet.

00:28:24.160 --> 00:28:25.770
So I'll play it again.

00:28:25.770 --> 00:28:28.530
And you see that depending
on what the magnetic field

00:28:28.530 --> 00:28:32.080
lines do, more and more of
these arcs can light up.

00:28:32.080 --> 00:28:34.810
And so you can imagine, if I
take the entire image together

00:28:34.810 --> 00:28:36.750
and say, how bright
is that image all over

00:28:36.750 --> 00:28:39.810
without knowing that, now
it's brighter than when

00:28:39.810 --> 00:28:40.763
I started the movie.

00:28:40.763 --> 00:28:42.180
So we can look at
the sun where we

00:28:42.180 --> 00:28:44.490
have actually a lot of
detail how these flares go

00:28:44.490 --> 00:28:46.260
and then we say,
OK, it's probably

00:28:46.260 --> 00:28:47.928
happening very
similar on other stars

00:28:47.928 --> 00:28:50.220
except other stars are brighter
and the magnetic fields

00:28:50.220 --> 00:28:54.880
are stronger and we
can compare them.

00:28:54.880 --> 00:28:56.960
Here's another one.

00:28:56.960 --> 00:28:58.880
So we call a coronal
mass ejection.

00:28:58.880 --> 00:29:01.010
It's stuff flying out
of the sun-- matter

00:29:01.010 --> 00:29:02.150
flying out of the sun.

00:29:02.150 --> 00:29:06.680
Now you might imagine if that
hits a planet, that might

00:29:06.680 --> 00:29:08.880
do something to the planet.

00:29:08.880 --> 00:29:11.750
[INAUDIBLE]

00:29:11.750 --> 00:29:13.400
In fact, it does.

00:29:13.400 --> 00:29:17.990
These particle and these
coronal mass ejections,

00:29:17.990 --> 00:29:20.612
when they hit the Earth, the
Earth has a magnetic field.

00:29:20.612 --> 00:29:22.070
And the magnetic
field of the Earth

00:29:22.070 --> 00:29:23.550
shields us for most of that.

00:29:23.550 --> 00:29:25.520
But the really strong
of those effects--

00:29:25.520 --> 00:29:27.110
the really strong
of those things,

00:29:27.110 --> 00:29:28.430
they can knock out satellites.

00:29:28.430 --> 00:29:29.972
They disrupt satellite
communication.

00:29:29.972 --> 00:29:31.970
If they were even
stronger, the strongest one

00:29:31.970 --> 00:29:34.490
that Earth has experienced is
called the Carrington Event

00:29:34.490 --> 00:29:37.520
after a guy named Carrington
who wrote a lot about it.

00:29:37.520 --> 00:29:40.790
That was in the
late 1800s, shortly

00:29:40.790 --> 00:29:43.160
after the US had the
first telegraphy lines.

00:29:43.160 --> 00:29:47.390
And the magnetic field wobbled
so much that the telegraph

00:29:47.390 --> 00:29:51.830
lines started giving sparks
in all directions and people

00:29:51.830 --> 00:29:54.503
got hurt from standing
close to the machines.

00:29:54.503 --> 00:29:56.420
Now, if you imagine doing
that to the internet

00:29:56.420 --> 00:29:58.040
today, where we are
far more connected

00:29:58.040 --> 00:30:01.310
and have far more data lines and
have electric wires everywhere,

00:30:01.310 --> 00:30:03.470
it's not clear what
that will do to us.

00:30:03.470 --> 00:30:05.090
We just hope it doesn't happen.

00:30:05.090 --> 00:30:06.300
That's an important part.

00:30:06.300 --> 00:30:07.980
But so understanding
stellar activity,

00:30:07.980 --> 00:30:10.120
how likely these events
are, how big they are,

00:30:10.120 --> 00:30:14.160
how can we prevent them does
have practical applications

00:30:14.160 --> 00:30:16.230
to us too.

00:30:16.230 --> 00:30:18.660
OK, another thing we can
do is we can do spectra.

00:30:18.660 --> 00:30:20.440
We can look at
spectra, another tool.

00:30:20.440 --> 00:30:22.920
And so spectra is
just the color--

00:30:22.920 --> 00:30:25.740
this is the spectrum of the sun.

00:30:25.740 --> 00:30:27.510
If you look at the
sun close enough

00:30:27.510 --> 00:30:29.910
and then you put it through
a prism or through--

00:30:29.910 --> 00:30:31.530
split it out in the rainbow.

00:30:31.530 --> 00:30:35.670
Or 10 years ago, I would
tell people to take a CD

00:30:35.670 --> 00:30:37.500
and sprinkle the
rainbows everywhere.

00:30:37.500 --> 00:30:39.150
If you look close
enough, you're going

00:30:39.150 --> 00:30:41.880
to find these black
lines in them.

00:30:41.880 --> 00:30:44.550
They're called Fraunhofer lines
after a guy named Fraunhofer

00:30:44.550 --> 00:30:45.780
who discovered them first.

00:30:45.780 --> 00:30:48.150
And it turns out
each of those lines

00:30:48.150 --> 00:30:50.140
is specific to a
specific element.

00:30:50.140 --> 00:30:53.910
So this one, for example, is
sodium in the orange here.

00:30:53.910 --> 00:30:58.080
And that has something to do
with how an atom has a nucleus

00:30:58.080 --> 00:31:00.750
and has electrons around it
and the electrons go like one

00:31:00.750 --> 00:31:02.190
level up or one level down.

00:31:02.190 --> 00:31:04.170
And that's a specific
change in energy.

00:31:04.170 --> 00:31:05.790
And every specific
change in energy

00:31:05.790 --> 00:31:08.550
is a specific color of light.

00:31:08.550 --> 00:31:10.440
Fortunately, we can
do the same experiment

00:31:10.440 --> 00:31:12.600
at home or in the lab
and we can measure which

00:31:12.600 --> 00:31:14.160
element goes with which color.

00:31:14.160 --> 00:31:16.080
And then, we can
say, oh, this star

00:31:16.080 --> 00:31:19.140
has a lot of very strong line--

00:31:19.140 --> 00:31:22.570
a very strong dip or vice
where we expect sodium.

00:31:22.570 --> 00:31:25.110
So this star must
have a lot of sodium.

00:31:25.110 --> 00:31:27.510
You can, in fact, do some of
those experiments at home.

00:31:27.510 --> 00:31:29.700
If you have a gas stove,
the flame is blue.

00:31:29.700 --> 00:31:33.000
And if you take some
sodium chloride or salt,

00:31:33.000 --> 00:31:34.770
if you take some salt
and dribble it in,

00:31:34.770 --> 00:31:36.900
the flame will become orange.

00:31:36.900 --> 00:31:39.162
It's a great
experiment for kids.

00:31:39.162 --> 00:31:41.370
I mean, an adult has to do
it because you have to get

00:31:41.370 --> 00:31:43.265
pretty close to the flame.

00:31:43.265 --> 00:31:44.640
But that's the
closest you'll see

00:31:44.640 --> 00:31:47.280
atomic physics in your kitchen.

00:31:47.280 --> 00:31:49.350
And so we do that for
a bunch of elements

00:31:49.350 --> 00:31:51.630
and compare that to what
we measure in the lab.

00:31:51.630 --> 00:31:54.280
And then, it can tell us what
elements are stars made of.

00:31:54.280 --> 00:31:56.160
And so we know
the sun has mostly

00:31:56.160 --> 00:31:59.490
hydrogen and has some sodium
and has iron and has lithium

00:31:59.490 --> 00:32:00.730
and has all sorts of things.

00:32:00.730 --> 00:32:03.997
And we can measure how much does
this star, have how much does

00:32:03.997 --> 00:32:06.330
that star have, does it change
with the age of the star,

00:32:06.330 --> 00:32:08.413
does it change with the
star having planets or not

00:32:08.413 --> 00:32:09.070
having planets?

00:32:09.070 --> 00:32:11.490
We can compare those things.

00:32:11.490 --> 00:32:13.580
This is a spectrum how
it looks on the X-rays.

00:32:13.580 --> 00:32:15.122
And there's other
elements and I just

00:32:15.122 --> 00:32:17.302
want to show you that
that's how they look.

00:32:17.302 --> 00:32:19.010
I don't want to go
through every new line

00:32:19.010 --> 00:32:19.760
and tell you what they do.

00:32:19.760 --> 00:32:21.440
I just want to
show you some data

00:32:21.440 --> 00:32:23.390
that I have personally
worked on so you

00:32:23.390 --> 00:32:25.430
get an idea of how it looks.

00:32:25.430 --> 00:32:28.377
That means most of my
work looks like this.

00:32:28.377 --> 00:32:29.960
Now, astronomers do
not wear lab coats

00:32:29.960 --> 00:32:31.460
and we don't work all at night.

00:32:31.460 --> 00:32:34.580
Very, very rarely people
actually use telescopes.

00:32:34.580 --> 00:32:37.070
I mostly use space
telescopes so I don't ever

00:32:37.070 --> 00:32:38.390
get to look through them.

00:32:38.390 --> 00:32:41.090
I don't ever get to go anywhere.

00:32:41.090 --> 00:32:43.940
People I work with, some of them
use ground based telescopes,

00:32:43.940 --> 00:32:45.380
like in Chile, for example.

00:32:45.380 --> 00:32:47.690
And fortunately, we don't
have to fly to Chile for that

00:32:47.690 --> 00:32:49.040
anymore.

00:32:49.040 --> 00:32:51.960
People used to fly to Hawaii to
use the telescopes in Hawaii.

00:32:51.960 --> 00:32:54.390
And while I would love to
go to Hawaii at some point,

00:32:54.390 --> 00:32:56.850
people tell me if you
go 10 times a year,

00:32:56.850 --> 00:32:59.150
it's not fun anymore.

00:32:59.150 --> 00:33:01.010
Because you fly out from here.

00:33:01.010 --> 00:33:02.780
You are 12 hours off.

00:33:02.780 --> 00:33:03.890
12 hours jetlagged, right?

00:33:03.890 --> 00:33:06.020
You have one day to
get used to Hawaii.

00:33:06.020 --> 00:33:08.910
Then, you get to the
bottom of the mountain,

00:33:08.910 --> 00:33:10.740
then you have a day to
get up the mountain.

00:33:10.740 --> 00:33:12.865
Then, you need to get
jetlagged by another 12 hours

00:33:12.865 --> 00:33:15.545
because now you suddenly
have to work at night.

00:33:15.545 --> 00:33:17.670
Because that's when you
use your telescopes, right?

00:33:17.670 --> 00:33:19.630
And then, you do it
all in reverse again.

00:33:19.630 --> 00:33:21.630
And so people would fly
to Hawaii or to Chile

00:33:21.630 --> 00:33:23.760
for just one night
of observations.

00:33:23.760 --> 00:33:26.200
And that's not just
expensive and a pain,

00:33:26.200 --> 00:33:28.260
it's also a huge
carbon emission.

00:33:28.260 --> 00:33:30.375
And I'm very glad we
have the internet today

00:33:30.375 --> 00:33:32.250
and don't have to do
that very often anymore.

00:33:32.250 --> 00:33:33.240
Sometimes you do.

00:33:33.240 --> 00:33:35.610
If you build your own
instrument with very specific

00:33:35.610 --> 00:33:37.860
instrumentation, you need to go
and screw it on the telescope,

00:33:37.860 --> 00:33:39.270
then you need to
be there, right?

00:33:39.270 --> 00:33:45.300
But for many cases, many
things can be done remotely.

00:33:45.300 --> 00:33:47.520
Most of the data, all
the data is from space.

00:33:47.520 --> 00:33:50.610
All the data NASA has taken
you can go and download.

00:33:50.610 --> 00:33:51.370
It's all public.

00:33:51.370 --> 00:33:51.990
It's all free.

00:33:51.990 --> 00:33:55.080
I'm not sure it's useful to you
because the file format might

00:33:55.080 --> 00:33:57.640
be a little bit obscure,
but you can do that.

00:33:57.640 --> 00:34:01.390
And so I work in an office.

00:34:01.390 --> 00:34:02.590
I write code.

00:34:02.590 --> 00:34:06.370
Most of my time, I've write
Python code I've write text.

00:34:06.370 --> 00:34:09.310
That's my job

00:34:09.310 --> 00:34:12.582
OK, specifically, for
space telescopes, I said--

00:34:12.582 --> 00:34:14.290
and I know I'm running
five minutes over,

00:34:14.290 --> 00:34:16.460
but I'm almost done.

00:34:16.460 --> 00:34:19.090
So the way Hubble
works or Chandra works,

00:34:19.090 --> 00:34:21.040
there's only one
Hubble and it's not

00:34:21.040 --> 00:34:22.405
owned by any particular person.

00:34:22.405 --> 00:34:23.530
So how do we get to use it?

00:34:23.530 --> 00:34:26.530
We essentially, once a
year, NASA writes proposals.

00:34:26.530 --> 00:34:29.199
So we write a letter that,
essentially, goes like this.

00:34:29.199 --> 00:34:32.020
Dear NASA, I would like
to look at IM loop.

00:34:32.020 --> 00:34:34.650
I think IM loop is
a really cool star.

00:34:34.650 --> 00:34:36.040
OK, a little better than that.

00:34:36.040 --> 00:34:39.310
IM loop is a star that
is older than-- younger

00:34:39.310 --> 00:34:42.460
than TW Hydra and that has
signs of a disk of accretion.

00:34:42.460 --> 00:34:44.420
We've looked at TW Hydra before.

00:34:44.420 --> 00:34:47.409
And we want to know if
what we've seen there,

00:34:47.409 --> 00:34:49.150
if that's true for
all young stars

00:34:49.150 --> 00:34:51.610
or only for stars that
are 10 million years old

00:34:51.610 --> 00:34:54.400
and if younger stars
behave slightly different.

00:34:54.400 --> 00:34:56.409
Then, I go that
and I send it in.

00:34:56.409 --> 00:34:58.810
And some group of people
is going to look at.

00:34:58.810 --> 00:35:01.940
That because every
year, NASA gets

00:35:01.940 --> 00:35:04.820
people asking for about nine
years or eight years of data.

00:35:04.820 --> 00:35:06.930
Now, there's only
365 nights a year.

00:35:06.930 --> 00:35:09.198
So there's only so much
you can do about it.

00:35:09.198 --> 00:35:11.240
There is some committee
that ranks them and says,

00:35:11.240 --> 00:35:13.520
this is the most likely
and this is a crap idea

00:35:13.520 --> 00:35:15.140
and this won't
work, technically.

00:35:15.140 --> 00:35:18.500
And this one we like, but we
just didn't have any time left.

00:35:18.500 --> 00:35:21.680
And so you send it in-- for
Chandra, you send it in March.

00:35:21.680 --> 00:35:24.170
Then, in June, you get an
email back that says, sorry,

00:35:24.170 --> 00:35:25.580
we're not going to do it.

00:35:25.580 --> 00:35:28.550
And we aren't going to do
it because you didn't really

00:35:28.550 --> 00:35:32.070
describe how long you need to
look or something like that.

00:35:32.070 --> 00:35:34.700
So next March in
the second year,

00:35:34.700 --> 00:35:36.150
you can update your proposal.

00:35:36.150 --> 00:35:38.330
You can send it in again
and explain that better.

00:35:38.330 --> 00:35:42.650
Let's say the second time
the committee accepts it.

00:35:42.650 --> 00:35:44.930
Now, that goes in
annual cycles, right?

00:35:44.930 --> 00:35:46.730
Your star isn't even
visible all the time

00:35:46.730 --> 00:35:49.147
because sometimes it's behind
the sun and stuff like that.

00:35:49.147 --> 00:35:51.290
So the stuff that gets
approved in June gets

00:35:51.290 --> 00:35:55.140
observed sometime between August
and the August the year after.

00:35:55.140 --> 00:35:58.020
So depending on how that
falls, in this particular case,

00:35:58.020 --> 00:36:00.050
it took-- even
after I sent it in,

00:36:00.050 --> 00:36:03.470
it took a year and two months
for me to get that data.

00:36:03.470 --> 00:36:08.450
Then, I take that data and I
observe it and I analyze it.

00:36:08.450 --> 00:36:09.950
Took me a couple
of months to think

00:36:09.950 --> 00:36:11.150
about what it really means.

00:36:11.150 --> 00:36:13.160
And then, I've write
it up in a journal.

00:36:13.160 --> 00:36:16.370
And now I go and say, OK,
maybe we need to look at a star

00:36:16.370 --> 00:36:19.310
that's even younger or that's
an age between IM loop and TW

00:36:19.310 --> 00:36:21.302
Hydra that has a higher
mass or lower mass

00:36:21.302 --> 00:36:22.260
or something like that.

00:36:22.260 --> 00:36:23.750
And then, I write
another proposal.

00:36:23.750 --> 00:36:24.830
Why do I tell you this?

00:36:24.830 --> 00:36:27.530
Well, A, that means a
timeline is very, very long

00:36:27.530 --> 00:36:29.050
between when you
start-- when you

00:36:29.050 --> 00:36:31.550
have an idea what you want to
look at until you actually do.

00:36:31.550 --> 00:36:34.220
It's even hard to squeeze
into a single PhD.

00:36:34.220 --> 00:36:36.110
And second, that's
why most of us

00:36:36.110 --> 00:36:38.660
work on at least a dozen
projects at the same time.

00:36:38.660 --> 00:36:41.000
Because after I wrote
this in year one,

00:36:41.000 --> 00:36:43.850
it's not like I twiddle my
thumb between March that year

00:36:43.850 --> 00:36:44.960
and the March next year.

00:36:44.960 --> 00:36:48.160
I need to have
other things to do.

00:36:48.160 --> 00:36:49.300
Said that already.

00:36:49.300 --> 00:36:51.670
OK, and then, we
try to interpret it.

00:36:51.670 --> 00:36:53.890
In this particular
case, this is IM loop.

00:36:53.890 --> 00:36:55.930
There's these flares
here in the UV

00:36:55.930 --> 00:36:57.700
and there's flags
in the X-rays here

00:36:57.700 --> 00:36:59.242
that suddenly becomes
a lot brighter.

00:36:59.242 --> 00:37:01.533
And so that tells us something
about the magnetic field

00:37:01.533 --> 00:37:02.200
of the star.

00:37:02.200 --> 00:37:04.340
And then, what do we
do with these results?

00:37:04.340 --> 00:37:07.420
Well, we tell people about it.

00:37:07.420 --> 00:37:10.280
One of the things we do is we
go to conferences where there'll

00:37:10.280 --> 00:37:12.142
be hundreds or
dozens of astronomers

00:37:12.142 --> 00:37:14.600
and they work on-- some of them
work on radio observations.

00:37:14.600 --> 00:37:16.350
Some of them work on
optical observations.

00:37:16.350 --> 00:37:17.545
And so you looked--

00:37:17.545 --> 00:37:19.000
I looked at IM
loop in the X-rays.

00:37:19.000 --> 00:37:20.668
You looked at IM
loop in the radio.

00:37:20.668 --> 00:37:22.210
You learned something
about the disk.

00:37:22.210 --> 00:37:23.668
I learned something
about the star.

00:37:23.668 --> 00:37:25.010
How does it fit together?

00:37:25.010 --> 00:37:27.007
So one of the
things people do is

00:37:27.007 --> 00:37:29.590
they give a talk, like I do now,
although usually not as long.

00:37:29.590 --> 00:37:31.180
It's often just 10
minutes or so because you

00:37:31.180 --> 00:37:32.740
don't have to explain
all the way around

00:37:32.740 --> 00:37:34.157
because everybody
there has worked

00:37:34.157 --> 00:37:36.490
on young stars for
at least five years.

00:37:36.490 --> 00:37:38.583
Or we make it a
poster where we just

00:37:38.583 --> 00:37:40.750
write the stuff so that you
can grab your colleagues

00:37:40.750 --> 00:37:43.390
and say, hey, come over
here come look what I did.

00:37:43.390 --> 00:37:44.810
And then, say, look what I did.

00:37:44.810 --> 00:37:46.600
And then, we hold it
next to each other.

00:37:46.600 --> 00:37:49.340
And then, ultimately, we
write it in a publication.

00:37:49.340 --> 00:37:51.780
We communicate a lot by
email with other people.

00:37:51.780 --> 00:37:55.130
And then, we write it into what
we call a publication, a paper.

00:37:55.130 --> 00:37:57.440
Although we call it paper,
although it's not on paper.

00:37:57.440 --> 00:37:58.890
None of them is printed anymore.

00:37:58.890 --> 00:38:00.307
They stop printing
five years ago.

00:38:00.307 --> 00:38:01.580
It's all electronic only.

00:38:01.580 --> 00:38:03.140
You have a question or remark?

00:38:03.140 --> 00:38:08.540
So when you get to use the
telescope for some amount

00:38:08.540 --> 00:38:11.250
of time, only you can use it.

00:38:11.250 --> 00:38:14.180
And that's because the--

00:38:14.180 --> 00:38:15.080
NASA

00:38:15.080 --> 00:38:18.860
Data you get is a very
small slice of the sky.

00:38:18.860 --> 00:38:20.720
For most telescopes.

00:38:20.720 --> 00:38:24.780
The answer depends a little bit
on the telescope, but usually.

00:38:24.780 --> 00:38:27.620
And so when I say use,
for the Chandra Space

00:38:27.620 --> 00:38:30.900
Telescope or Hubble, what I do
is I fill a web form that says,

00:38:30.900 --> 00:38:34.230
look at this star this
camera for that long.

00:38:34.230 --> 00:38:35.870
And then, I don't
actually have to know

00:38:35.870 --> 00:38:36.800
where that star is in the sky.

00:38:36.800 --> 00:38:38.210
I can't tell you where IM lupus.

00:38:38.210 --> 00:38:39.023
It's in lupus.

00:38:39.023 --> 00:38:40.190
I don't know where lupus is.

00:38:40.190 --> 00:38:42.315
It's probably in the Southern
hemisphere somewhere.

00:38:42.315 --> 00:38:43.980
I think it is.

00:38:43.980 --> 00:38:45.300
I hope it is.

00:38:45.300 --> 00:38:47.430
But somebody else
takes care of that

00:38:47.430 --> 00:38:50.220
because it's not like any
random person can use a joystick

00:38:50.220 --> 00:38:51.948
and move Hubble around, right?

00:38:51.948 --> 00:38:54.240
This ground-based telescope,
that's a little different.

00:38:54.240 --> 00:38:55.655
Specifically, if you go--

00:38:55.655 --> 00:38:57.280
you are a little
closer to the controls

00:38:57.280 --> 00:38:59.697
and you actually press a button
and make the thing rotate.

00:39:03.890 --> 00:39:07.280
OK, so just before
the end, what can you

00:39:07.280 --> 00:39:09.690
do if you care for astronomy?

00:39:09.690 --> 00:39:11.780
Well, one important
thing is to be excited.

00:39:11.780 --> 00:39:12.650
To like it.

00:39:12.650 --> 00:39:15.530
To listen to me and other people
and find out what it's about.

00:39:15.530 --> 00:39:18.170
You can observe
the sky yourself.

00:39:18.170 --> 00:39:20.873
We greatly appreciate
if the sky stays dark

00:39:20.873 --> 00:39:22.790
because it's much easier
to see when it's dark

00:39:22.790 --> 00:39:24.290
outside than when it's light.

00:39:24.290 --> 00:39:27.530
So for example, around
in Arizona, there's

00:39:27.530 --> 00:39:30.050
an observatory on
Kitt Peak that used

00:39:30.050 --> 00:39:32.030
to be a fantastically dark sky.

00:39:32.030 --> 00:39:36.140
But suburban developments, and
in particular, big box shopping

00:39:36.140 --> 00:39:38.300
malls are coming closer
and closer and closer.

00:39:38.300 --> 00:39:40.220
And for some reason,
all those parking lots

00:39:40.220 --> 00:39:42.020
need to be lit 24/7.

00:39:42.020 --> 00:39:44.720
And most of them are lit
with lights that don't just

00:39:44.720 --> 00:39:46.220
shine down that also shine up.

00:39:46.220 --> 00:39:48.110
That's a problem.

00:39:48.110 --> 00:39:51.340
More recently, Starlink.

00:39:51.340 --> 00:39:54.610
If you want to launch 20,000
satellites in the sky,

00:39:54.610 --> 00:39:56.080
they're going to
visceral pictures.

00:39:56.080 --> 00:39:57.700
They already do.

00:39:57.700 --> 00:39:59.980
On X-rays we are fine because
they don't emit X-rays.

00:39:59.980 --> 00:40:02.230
And also, our X-ray satellites
are even further out.

00:40:02.230 --> 00:40:04.120
But with optical
telescope, you constantly

00:40:04.120 --> 00:40:06.610
have images where there's
just streaks of right

00:40:06.610 --> 00:40:10.750
through because there's all
these satellites going through.

00:40:10.750 --> 00:40:12.100
Pay taxes, right?

00:40:12.100 --> 00:40:13.910
Because that's what
pays our salary.

00:40:13.910 --> 00:40:15.880
That's what-- I said
satellites are expensive.

00:40:15.880 --> 00:40:20.560
And there's amateur astronomers,
who we sometimes work with.

00:40:20.560 --> 00:40:23.530
This is one as RW Raj's star.

00:40:23.530 --> 00:40:24.952
And this is, again,
a light curve

00:40:24.952 --> 00:40:27.160
in different bands, which
is why the different colors

00:40:27.160 --> 00:40:28.300
and different filters.

00:40:28.300 --> 00:40:31.510
And so those are people who just
are interested in astronomy.

00:40:31.510 --> 00:40:33.308
They observe this star.

00:40:33.308 --> 00:40:35.350
Some of them every night,
some of them only every

00:40:35.350 --> 00:40:35.990
now and then.

00:40:35.990 --> 00:40:37.990
And then, the upload how
bright it was when they

00:40:37.990 --> 00:40:40.190
looked at it in that database.

00:40:40.190 --> 00:40:42.200
And even years later,
I can go and say,

00:40:42.200 --> 00:40:44.600
now I have something interesting
about how [INAUDIBLE]..

00:40:44.600 --> 00:40:47.400
I wonder what it did in the
last couple of years before?

00:40:47.400 --> 00:40:51.680
And I pull this data and
say, oh, this isn't actually

00:40:51.680 --> 00:40:54.500
the first time it
suddenly became dimmer.

00:40:54.500 --> 00:40:57.050
It already went
very dim over here.

00:40:57.050 --> 00:40:59.220
I wonder what that means?

00:40:59.220 --> 00:41:02.960
But if nobody looks,
I wouldn't have known.

00:41:02.960 --> 00:41:05.170
There's also things
you can go through--

00:41:05.170 --> 00:41:06.730
so other astronomical
data and help

00:41:06.730 --> 00:41:09.560
like look at classified like
on an iPhone app and say,

00:41:09.560 --> 00:41:11.830
this looks like two stars.

00:41:11.830 --> 00:41:15.285
There's one over here
and one over here.

00:41:15.285 --> 00:41:17.160
Whereas, in other
pictures, there's only one.

00:41:17.160 --> 00:41:18.610
And why does it matter?

00:41:18.610 --> 00:41:19.920
Well, there's a good camera.

00:41:19.920 --> 00:41:22.080
We can see it's two pin
stars as good resolution.

00:41:22.080 --> 00:41:24.570
If you look at it with the
infrared telescope where

00:41:24.570 --> 00:41:26.220
everything is a little
more washed out,

00:41:26.220 --> 00:41:28.883
you might-- these two
stars might shine together.

00:41:28.883 --> 00:41:31.050
And so you see it's bright,
but it's actually bright

00:41:31.050 --> 00:41:32.008
because it's two stars.

00:41:32.008 --> 00:41:33.570
It's not because
the one is bright.

00:41:33.570 --> 00:41:35.610
And so there's
programs like that

00:41:35.610 --> 00:41:38.760
where you can do observations
or look at observations if you

00:41:38.760 --> 00:41:40.560
want, OK?

00:41:40.560 --> 00:41:43.050
Stars form in large
molecular clouds.

00:41:43.050 --> 00:41:44.730
Young stars are
surrounded by disks.

00:41:44.730 --> 00:41:47.710
And these disks form planets,
or the stars form planets,

00:41:47.710 --> 00:41:48.210
we don't.

00:41:48.210 --> 00:41:50.070
But we observe planet formation.

00:41:50.070 --> 00:41:52.590
Young stars have these
powerful outflows, these jets

00:41:52.590 --> 00:41:54.780
that drive stuff into space.

00:41:54.780 --> 00:41:57.210
Star formation happens
right now in the Milky Way.

00:41:57.210 --> 00:41:57.802
It's not over.

00:41:57.802 --> 00:41:59.760
Even though our Earth is
old, stars and planets

00:41:59.760 --> 00:42:01.350
are still forming
almost everywhere.

00:42:01.350 --> 00:42:02.958
And we do many
different observations.

00:42:02.958 --> 00:42:04.500
We use different
telescopes so we can

00:42:04.500 --> 00:42:05.460
look at different wavelengths.

00:42:05.460 --> 00:42:07.668
We can look at different
aspects to better understand

00:42:07.668 --> 00:42:08.430
how that works.

00:42:08.430 --> 00:42:10.740
Are you able to see stars
forming in other galaxies

00:42:10.740 --> 00:42:11.430
as well?

00:42:11.430 --> 00:42:12.630
Yes.

00:42:12.630 --> 00:42:14.370
I mean, I don't
because I specialize

00:42:14.370 --> 00:42:15.760
in stars in our galaxy.

00:42:15.760 --> 00:42:19.240
And in other galaxies,
they're just so far away

00:42:19.240 --> 00:42:20.360
that it's hard to see.

00:42:20.360 --> 00:42:22.360
So in other galaxies, you
only see the brightest

00:42:22.360 --> 00:42:23.720
and most massive stars.

00:42:23.720 --> 00:42:26.590
Things like our sun you wouldn't
see in a different galaxy.

00:42:26.590 --> 00:42:30.040
But things that are hundreds
times as massive in the sun you

00:42:30.040 --> 00:42:30.670
would.

00:42:30.670 --> 00:42:33.595
So things like our sun you
don't see individually.

